---
/**
 * Emergency — water in the basement, EN. Twin of `src/pages/urgence.astro`.
 *
 * The page most exposed to rule 1bis on the whole site. Someone with water in
 * the basement right now wants to know what to do, and that is exactly what
 * cannot be given: no pump, no tarp, no "temporary fix in the meantime" — that
 * last one is named and forbidden in docs/CLAUDE.md.
 *
 * Every action suggested here is observing, documenting or telephoning.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pathFor } from '@lib/routes.ts';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@lib/schema.ts';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const ref = { type: 'static', page: 'emergency' } as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  ref={ref}
  title="Water in the basement — what to do right now"
  description="Water in the basement after rain or snowmelt: the real dangers to know, who to call depending on the cause, and what protects your insurance claim."
  schema={[
    breadcrumbSchema(locale, [
      { name: "Home", ref: { type: 'home' } },
      { name: "Emergency", ref },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <section class="contenant bloc-page">
    <div class="mesure">
      <h1>Water in your basement right now</h1>

      <p class="chapeau">
        This page will not tell you how to tackle it yourself, and that is deliberate. It tells
        you what is dangerous, who to telephone depending on the cause, and what to do
        immediately so that you do not lose a claim.
      </p>

      <h2>First: electricity</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Do not enter a flooded room if the water is touching or approaching an outlet, an
        extension cord, a plugged-in appliance or the electrical panel.</strong> There is nothing
        to attempt in that situation: leave the room and call a master electrician, or
        Hydro-Québec if the electrical service entrance is involved. This is the one emergency on
        this page that puts people at risk rather than a building.
      </p>

      <h2>Then: where is the water coming from?</h2>
      <p>
        The answer decides who to call, and above all who pays. There are three cases, and they
        do not share a phone number.
      </p>

      <h3>It is coming up through a floor drain or a toilet</h3>
      <p>
        That is a sewer backup, and it belongs to the municipal network, not to a foundation
        contractor. <strong>Report it to your city</strong> — 311 for Ville de Québec — before
        committing to anything. The city determines whether the cause is on its portion of the
        network or on yours, and that answer changes who bears the cost.
      </p>

      <h3>Water is entering at the base of a wall, a joint or a crack</h3>
      <p>
        That points at the foundation itself: a drain that no longer carries water, a crack that
        has opened, a membrane at the end of its life. That is this site's subject. The first
        useful step is not excavation, it is a diagnosis — see
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'service', service: 'infiltration' }, locale)}>basement water
        infiltration</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>It is a pipe, a water heater or an appliance</h3>
      <p>
        That is not a foundation problem. It is a plumber, and your insurer.
      </p>

      <h2>Call your insurer early, not later</h2>
      <p>
        Water damage has to be reported promptly, and most policies require it. Before throwing
        anything out or replacing it, ask your insurer what they need to see: belongings disposed
        of before they are documented can become impossible to claim for.
      </p>

      <h2>Document it while it is still visible</h2>
      <p>
        The water goes, and the evidence with it. While the situation is in front of you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>photograph the exact point where water is entering, not just the puddle;</li>
        <li>note the date, the time and the weather — heavy rain, snowmelt, or nothing unusual;</li>
        <li>
          if you can see a crack, photograph it with a ruler beside it, so the scale can be
          verified later;
        </li>
        <li>keep the invoices for everything that follows.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That file is what will let you compare two quotes against the same observation, and what
        holds a claim together.
      </p>

      <h2>If the building is under five years old</h2>
      <p>
        Do not have it repaired before checking your coverage. The new residential building
        warranty plan covers five years for design, construction or execution defects and soil
        defects — the window that reaches a foundation. <strong>Having the work done yourself can
        compromise the claim</strong>, because the evidence disappears along with the wall.
        Report it first. The <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'faq' }, locale)}>frequently
        asked questions</a> set out the deadlines.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this page suggests no quick fix</h2>
      <p>
        Because there is no safe, generic action to take on a foundation that is letting water
        in, and a badly chosen "temporary fix" usually moves the problem rather than stopping it.
        This site explains the phenomena and the regulations; the intervention belongs to a
        contractor holding the right licence subcategories.
      </p>

      <p class="cta-fin">
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>Request a quote</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
